# nomo snapshot v1
# worksheet: checks

=== text ===
# Verdicts — a design that states whether it holds

A worksheet is not finished when it has computed a number. It is finished when
it says whether the number is acceptable, and against what. That last step is
usually the point of the document: a section is adequate, a bolt is not, a
temperature rise is within what the datasheet allows.

`check` states a condition and reports a verdict on it. It binds nothing and
nothing reads it — it exists to be read by a person, and counted by whatever
runs the worksheet.
## A bracket in bending
unit kip = 1000 lbf
M = 4.2 kip·in
S = 0.42 in³
Fy = 36 ksi
The allowable stress, and the stress that is actually there.
sigma_allow = 0.6·Fy = 0.6·(36 ksi) = 148927000 Pa
sigma = M/S = 474.536 J/(6.88257e-6 m³) = 68947600 Pa
sigma = 10 ksi
sigma_allow = 21.6 ksi
check sigma ≤ sigma_allow = 68947600 Pa ≤ 148927000 Pa — pass
## Deflection, against a span/360 limit
L = 48 in
delta = 0.09 in
delta_allow = L/360 = 48 in/360 = 0.00338667 m
delta_allow = 0.133333 in
check delta ≤ delta_allow = 0.09 in ≤ 0.00338667 m — pass
## A check may be any condition, not only a comparison
Both of these have to hold, and one statement says so.
t = 0.25 in
t_min = 0.1875 in
check t ≥ t_min and delta ≤ delta_allow = 0.25 in ≥ 0.1875 in and 0.09 in ≤ 0.00338667 m — pass
## A verdict that does not hold
Nothing here is wrong: the arithmetic is right and the design is not. The
worksheet still evaluates, `nomo check` still reports it as a worksheet that
works, and the exit code says a check failed rather than that the sheet did.
d_bolt = 0.375 in
d_required = 0.5 in
check d_bolt ≥ d_required = 0.375 in ≥ 0.5 in — FAIL

